  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThursday...any lingering precipitation across northeast MO, west \ncentral IL, north central IL will push southeast early AM, as high \npressure moves into the region. Some CAMs suggest some showers \nwill be possible across north central IL during the afternoon \nhours, but coverage will be quite isolated if they occur. Highs \nto top out in the lower 80s area-wide.\n\nFriday...Isolated to scattered storms (20-40% coverage) are back on \nthe table as a surface trough moves through the area. Moisture \navailability and limited instability due to the lack of prolonged \nreturn flow and WAA should dampen any severe threat with these \nstorms. By Friday night, the boundary clears the CWA as another weak \nhigh pressure moves in.\n\nSaturday...A rinse-and-repeat of Thursday is likely on Saturday\nas convergence aloft on the backside of Friday's shortwave trough \nand low-level CAA keep things dry. Seasonable highs in the low to \nmid 80s is forecast.\n\nSunday-Tuesday...active weather pattern returns next week with \nseveral shortwaves moving through. There remains a lot of \nuncertainty in PoPs heading into early next week as persistent west-\nnorthwesterly flow aloft prevents any significant moisture advection \ninto the region. What is a little more certain is the shift in low-\nlevel flow. Another weak shortwave is forecast to deepen a low \npressure on the leeward side of the Northern Rockies and move \neastward towards Iowa and Minnesota on Monday. General consensus \namong ensemble models is a southerly wind shift which will kick off \na warming trend Sunday and into early next week with highs likely \n(60-80% chance per 04.12z LREF guidance) in the mid-80s to low 90s. \nThese temperatures combined with dewpoints in the 70s will result in \nafternoon heat indices peaking in the upper 90s/low 100s along and \nsouth of I-80 Sunday and Monday. Heat headlines may eventually be \nneeded at some point for a portion of the area. In addition, models \nare beginning to show an elevated theta-e boundary setting up Monday \nnight-Tuesday across IA, that may become a trigger for storm \ndevelopment overnight. Any residual boundary left across the region \non Tuesday will also play a huge role on the northward expansion of \nthe heat.",
